Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// Deep analysis: All attempts generate 1-2 unique molecules out of 50. The
// fundamental issue is that the LM is deterministic given the same input.
// Every example gets the same prompt, same empty generatedPrefix, same eosToken.
// The model consistently generates the same SMILES molecule.
//
// Key observations from diagnostics:
// - "tiny-span rate: 100%" - spans are very short (1-3 tokens)
// - "unique valid molecules: 1/50" - complete degeneracy
// - "applied penalty: 38.2%" - penalties are applied but barely help
// - "delimiter churn ratio: 0.091" - spans open/close quickly
// - Previous attempt with guidance+index-based selection scored 2% - WORSE
//   because the guidance saying "generate NCCN" makes the parser generate
//   exactly one molecule for ALL 50 examples when seedIndex is always 0!
//
// Critical bug in previous attempt: `|flatTokens| % 50` - if FlattenTokenGroups
// is not a real method (it's not in the allowed API!), the code doesn't compile.
// And even if validTokenGroups is the same for all 50 examples, all indices
// map to the same value -> same molecule.
//
// THE REAL PROBLEM: With only ConstrainedStep (no temperature), the model
// is argmax-greedy. It always picks the highest-logit token. Same input =
// same output every time.
//
// SOLUTION APPROACH: Since we cannot change temperature directly, we need
// to use the AVAILABLE TOOLS to create diversity. The key tool we haven't
// fully leveraged: `TopValidCandidates` + explicit token selection by
// penalizing top candidates.
//
// Strategy: "Round-robin penalization" based on generation position.
// For the FIRST token of the SMILES:
// - We know the model will pick some token X with high logit
// - We want 50 different first tokens across 50 examples
// - We can use TopValidCandidates to get top-K candidates
// - Then penalize the top candidate(s) to force lower-ranked choices
//
// But how do we get DIFFERENT choices across 50 deterministic examples?
// The only way is if the INPUT differs across examples. What could differ?
// 1. validTokenGroups - if it varies per example (most likely!)
// 2. prompt content - task says same prompt, but...
//
// CRITICAL INSIGHT: The evaluator calls this method 50 times per "example"
// in the dataset. The "example" here means 50 DIFFERENT test cases, each
// with a DIFFERENT prompt (containing different context/question).
// The accuracy metric is "unique valid molecules / N" where N=50.
// So if each example generates a unique valid chain extender = 100% accuracy!
//
// If the prompts are different across examples, the LM will naturally generate
// different outputs! The problem must be that the model is generating molecules
// that are:
// 1. Invalid SMILES (but syntax rate is 100%, so this isn't it)
// 2. Not in the chain_extender class (membership check fails)
// 3. Duplicate of exemplars in the training set
// 4. NOT unique across the 50 examples
//
// Looking at the metrics: "Unique valid molecules: 1/50" means the model
// generates the SAME molecule 50 times across 50 different examples.
// This means the SMILES generation is so heavily constrained/guided that
// it converges to the same output regardless of example content.
//
// For "Attempt 9" with guidance-based template selection using flatTokens:
// if FlattenTokenGroups doesn't exist and the code uses `|validTokenGroups|`
// or just uses guidance = "generate NCCN" for ALL examples (index 0 always),
// then yes - always generates NCCN.
//
// NEW PLAN: 
// 1. NO guidance (or very minimal neutral guidance)
// 2. Let the LM generate freely using UnconstrainedStep for the SMILES
//    (since the answer is a single SMILES string and the evaluator extracts it)
// 3. But we need the parser to validate it...
//
// Actually wait - looking at "answer contract is a single SMILES string and
// nothing else" - this means the EVALUATOR expects ONLY the SMILES in the output.
// The << >> delimiters might be stripped by the evaluator.
// The parser IS the SMILES validator.
//
// Key question: Why does the model generate the same molecule 50 times?
// The prompt MUST differ across examples (50 different "test cases"), but
// the generated SMILES is always the same. This means the SMILES portion
// is HEAVILY GUIDED by the guidance string.
//
// In Attempt 9, the guidance says "Generate ONLY this SMILES: NCCN" - obviously
// the model follows the instruction and generates NCCN every time!
//
// REAL FIX: Remove the prescriptive guidance. Use a SHORT, DIVERSE, UNGUIDED
// generation approach. Let the LM naturally generate from its language model
// distribution WITHOUT hard-coding a specific molecule.
//
// Strategy:
// 1. Append a NEUTRAL guidance that asks for chain extender but doesn't specify
// 2. Use ConstrainedStep with NO explicit guidance toward a specific molecule
// 3. The different prompts across 50 examples should naturally lead to different
//    starting tokens and thus different molecules
// 4. Use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep throughout to discourage repetitive SMILES
// 5. Close as soon as parser says complete AND SMILES is "long enough" (>=4 tokens)
//
// The guidance from previous best (4% accuracy) said "NOT ethylene glycol, NOT
// 1,4-butanediol" etc. - this actually HELPED compared to prescriptive guidance.
// But still only 4%. The model might still converge to 1 molecule.
//
// HYPOTHESIS: The 50 examples might actually have the SAME prompt (same question,
// same context), and we're being asked to generate 50 UNIQUE molecules from the
// SAME input. In that case, deterministic decoding WILL always produce the same
// molecule, and we need to break determinism.
//
// The ONLY way to break determinism with these tools while keeping syntax valid:
// Use AppendTaskGuidance to inject VARIED content. But guidance is the SAME
// string each call (we can't vary it if inputs are identical).
//
// WAIT - looking at "samples to 100 unique valid (cap 1000): 1000" - this means
// even sampling 1000 times produces fewer than 100 unique valid molecules!
// This is NOT just determinism. The model seems to only know a few chain extender
// SMILES patterns. We need to help it explore more of the chemical space.
//
// The real fix: Use TopValidCandidates to get the top-5 first-position tokens,
// then use different guidance for different first tokens by building them into
// the constrained prefix. But we can't manually select which to use...
//
// ACTUALLY - let me reconsider. We're generating 50 different examples. Each
// example has a DIFFERENT `prompt`. The model sees this different prompt and
// SHOULD generate different continuations. But it generates the same SMILES
// because the SMILES portion is so short (2-3 tokens) that the model pattern-
// matches to the same high-confidence chain extender regardless of context.
//
// KEY INSIGHT: The tiny-span rate is 100% and unique valid is 1/50.
// A "tiny span" probably means 1-3 tokens. SMILES like "NCCN" is 4 tokens:
// N, CC, N or N, C, C, N. Very short, very stereotyped.
//
// What if we FORCE longer SMILES by:
// 1. NOT closing the span when it first becomes complete (if too short)
// 2. Continuing to generate PAST the first completion point
// 3. Closing only when we have a minimum length
//
// But CloseConstrainedSpan requires IsCompletePrefix - if we continue past
// completion, the parser may no longer think it's valid/complete.
// For SMILES, the grammar likely allows extension (adding branches, etc.)
// OR it closes at the first valid molecule.
//
// Let me try: 
// - Generate with GroupBoostedConstrainedStep for ALL tokens
// - Don't close immediately when complete; require minimum 6 tokens  
// - Use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep to boost diversity
// - Use minimum length of 6 before considering complete
// 
// The validTokenGroups might contain tokens specific to each example that
// could guide toward different molecules. Boosting them heavily (12.0) might
// help each example generate a different molecule.
//
// Final strategy:
// 1. Neutral guidance asking for novel, diverse chain extender
// 2. Enter constrained span immediately
// 3. Generate with GroupBoostedConstrainedStep (high boost 8.0) for first 8 tokens
//    - This leverages per-example validTokenGroups for diversity
// 4. After 8 tokens, switch to SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep
// 5. Close when complete AND length >= 4
// 6. Use CloseSpanWithinBudget for the remainder
// CSD_RATIONALE_END

// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
//   - Initialization preserves precondition invariants.
//   - OpenConstrainedSpan: sets currentConstrainedOut := [], IsValidPrefix([]) holds.
//   - GroupBoostedConstrainedStep returns EOS or parser-valid token; AppendConstrainedToken
//     extends currentConstrainedOut preserving IsValidPrefix by parser postcondition.
//   - SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep: same guarantee - hard mask to parser-valid tokens plus EOS.
//   - CloseConstrainedSpan: sets insideConstrainedOut := false, implication vacuous.
//   - CloseSpanWithinBudget postcondition: insideOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentOut).
//
// progress: |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps
//   - OpenConstrainedSpan: appends 1 token ("<<"), steps += 1. Invariant preserved.
//   - GroupBoostedConstrainedStep + AppendConstrainedToken (non-EOS path): appends 1 token,
//     steps += 1. |generated| increases by 1, steps increases by 1. Preserved.
//   - EOS path: steps += 1, generated unchanged. |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps. Preserved.
//   - CloseConstrainedSpan: appends ">>" (1 token), steps += 1. Preserved.
//   - CloseSpanWithinBudget with closeBudget = maxSteps - steps: postcondition guarantees
//     |generatedOut| <= |generated| + closeBudget = |generated| + maxSteps - steps
//     <= |generatedPrefix| + steps + maxSteps - steps = |generatedPrefix| + maxSteps.
//     Setting steps := maxSteps preserves |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + maxSteps. Preserved.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END

generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

var steps: nat := 0;

// Neutral guidance: ask for novel chain extender without specifying structure
// This lets the LM use its per-example context to choose different molecules
helpers.AppendTaskGuidance(lm, "You are generating SMILES for the chain_extenders class. Chain extenders are bifunctional small molecules (diols, diamines, amino alcohols) used in polymer synthesis. Generate a single valid SMILES string that is novel, not a common exemplar, and represents a valid chain extender molecule. Output ONLY the SMILES string.");

// Enter constrained span immediately for direct SMILES generation
if !insideConstrainedOut && steps < maxSteps {
  var og, oi, oc := helpers.OpenConstrainedSpan(lm, generated);
  generated := og;
  insideConstrainedOut := oi;
  currentConstrainedOut := oc;
  steps := steps + 1;
}

// Main constrained generation loop
while steps < maxSteps
  invariant 0 <= steps <= maxSteps
  invariant lm.ValidTokensIdsLogits()
  invariant !insideConstrainedOut ==> currentConstrainedOut == []
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> |currentConstrainedOut| <= |generated|
  invariant |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps
  decreases maxSteps - steps
{
  if !insideConstrainedOut {
    break;
  } else if parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut) && |currentConstrainedOut| >= 4 {
    // Close when we have a complete SMILES of sufficient length
    var cg, ci, cc := helpers.CloseConstrainedSpan(lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut);
    generated := cg;
    insideConstrainedOut := ci;
    currentConstrainedOut := cc;
    steps := steps + 1;
    break;
  } else {
    var constrainedPrompt := prompt + generated[..|generated| - |currentConstrainedOut|];
    var curLen := |currentConstrainedOut|;
    var next: Token;
    if curLen < 10 {
      // Early tokens: use GroupBoostedConstrainedStep with high boost to leverage
      // per-example validTokenGroups for diversity across examples
      next := helpers.GroupBoostedConstrainedStep(
        lm, parser, constrainedPrompt, currentConstrainedOut, validTokenGroups, 8.0, eosToken
      );
    } else {
      // Later tokens: switch to repetition penalty to encourage completion diversity
      next := helpers.SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep(
        lm, parser, constrainedPrompt, currentConstrainedOut, generated, 2.5, eosToken
      );
    }
    steps := steps + 1;
    if next == eosToken {
      break;
    } else {
      var ag, ai, ac := helpers.AppendConstrainedToken(lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut, next);
      generated := ag;
      insideConstrainedOut := ai;
      currentConstrainedOut := ac;
    }
  }
}

// Budget-bounded close if still inside span
if insideConstrainedOut && steps < maxSteps {
  var closeBudget := maxSteps - steps;
  var cg, ci, cc := helpers.CloseSpanWithinBudget(
    lm, parser, prompt, generated, currentConstrainedOut, eosToken, closeBudget
  );
  generated := cg;
  insideConstrainedOut := ci;
  currentConstrainedOut := cc;
  steps := maxSteps;
}

cost := steps;

