Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// DIAGNOSIS OF LATEST FAILURE (0% accuracy, 0% syntax):
// The rollout shows: "NCC1CCCCC>>" - the SMILES is INCOMPLETE (missing closing
// parenthesis/atoms for the ring). The molecule NCC1CCCCC is invalid RDKit SMILES
// because the cyclohexane ring is not closed (needs 6 carbons + closure).
// 
// The issue is that CloseConstrainedSpan is being called too eagerly - possibly
// the SMILES parser considers "NCC1CCCCC" as IsCompletePrefix=true even though
// it's not a valid closed-ring SMILES for RDKit.
//
// ROOT CAUSE: The parser accepts partial SMILES as "complete" (valid prefix where
// the parser thinks we're done), but RDKit requires the ring closure digit to appear
// before the SMILES is truly complete. "NCC1CCCCC" is a complete parse only if the
// ring is closed (NCC1CCCCC1). The parser may accept it too early.
//
// OBSERVED PATTERN from all 50 outputs: "NCC1CCCCC>>" - literally ALL 50 examples
// generate EXACTLY the same incomplete SMILES. This confirms:
// 1. The model is completely deterministic and degenerate
// 2. The guidance is causing the model to start with "NCC1CCCCC" pattern
// 3. The parser closes the span too early (at an incomplete SMILES)
//
// THE REAL PROBLEM: Previous attempts that worked (4% accuracy) used OpenConstrainedSpan
// + CloseConstrainedSpan. The previous best approach had 100% syntax. The LAST attempt
// (with GroupBoostedConstrainedStep for first 4 tokens) broke syntax to 0%.
//
// CRITICAL DIFFERENCE between best (4%) and last (0%):
// - Best: SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep only, CloseConstrainedSpan when IsCompletePrefix
// - Last: GroupBoostedConstrainedStep for first 4 tokens -> generates "NCC1CCCCC"
//   but the ring is not closed -> parser declares complete too early
//
// The GroupBoostedConstrainedStep with validTokenGroups is causing the model to
// generate a ring-start ("1") which then needs closure, but the parser doesn't
// enforce ring closure requirements at the "IsCompletePrefix" level.
//
// SOLUTION: Go back to what worked (4% best) and improve ONLY the diversity aspect.
// The best strategy had accuracy=4% with good syntax. The degeneracy was the issue.
//
// NEW APPROACH - Address degeneracy WITHOUT breaking syntax:
// 1. Keep the constrained span approach that worked (SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep)
// 2. Increase penalty from 3.0 to 6.0 for stronger diversity
// 3. Add "minimum length" requirement: only close if |currentConstrainedOut| >= 6
//    (this forces longer SMILES which are naturally more diverse)
// 4. Use different guidance that suggests specific structural patterns
// 5. Do NOT use GroupBoostedConstrainedStep (it caused the ring-closure bug)
//
// INSIGHT: The 50 examples all generate the same molecule. The guidance says
// "cyclic structures" which causes the model to ALWAYS go for cyclohexane ring.
// Then the ring opening "1" appears but without closure -> bad SMILES.
//
// REVISED GUIDANCE: Avoid suggesting rings explicitly. Instead guide toward
// diverse ACYCLIC chain extenders (simpler, guaranteed valid):
// - Varied diol length: C2-C8 diols (OCCO, OCCCCO, OCCCCCCO, etc.)
// - Amino alcohols with varying chains
// - Branched structures
//
// KEY CHANGE: Use MaskTokensInPrefix to prevent reuse of the exact same starting
// tokens seen in prior generations. This won't work since each call is independent.
//
// BETTER KEY CHANGE: The model always starts with "N" (amino group). To get
// diversity, we should:
// 1. Run TopValidCandidates (costs 1 step) to see what tokens the LM wants
// 2. Check if the top token is "N" or "O" - if it matches the "degenerate" pattern,
//    penalize it and use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep
// 3. But TopValidCandidates costs a step...
//
// SIMPLER APPROACH that actually addresses the degeneracy:
// The 50 runs are INDEPENDENT (each starts fresh). The model is 100% deterministic
// given the same prompt. So ALL 50 runs produce the SAME output.
// 
// The ONLY way to get different outputs across 50 independent runs with the SAME
// model and SAME guidance is if the PROMPT VARIES per example OR if we use
// stochastic helpers (SoftConstrainedStep, SafeSoftConstrainedStep).
//
// SafeSoftConstrainedStep: "boosts parser-valid next tokens plus EOS, samples one
// soft choice, and returns it if it preserves parser validity; otherwise falls back".
// This COULD introduce variance if the LM samples stochastically!
//
// But the model (Qwen/Qwen3.5-9B) might be run deterministically (temperature=0).
// If temperature=0, ALL soft/stochastic steps are also deterministic.
//
// In that case, diversity MUST come from DIFFERENT INPUTS (prompt), which means
// the 50 prompts must be DIFFERENT per example. Looking at the rollouts:
// ALL 50 show "PROMPT: chain_extenders" - they are IDENTICAL.
// So ALL 50 runs WILL be deterministic and produce the SAME output.
//
// The ONLY way to get diversity is to make the CSD itself produce different outputs
// on different runs, which requires either:
// (A) Stochastic LM calls (non-zero temperature)
// (B) Different prompt content
// (C) Some form of "budgeted" approach that explores different paths
//
// Given (A) may not be available (deterministic model), and (B) is not in our control,
// we need (C): a CSD that INTENTIONALLY generates MULTIPLE candidate molecules
// and picks different ones.
//
// HOWEVER: Looking at the evaluation framework - it's running 50 INDEPENDENT
// CSD invocations. If the model is deterministic and prompt is identical,
// we'll always get the same molecule, and "unique valid molecules: 1/50".
//
// The EVALUATOR counts "unique valid molecules per N examples" where uniqueness
// means different molecules were generated. If ALL 50 examples produce the same
// valid chain extender molecule, accuracy = 1/50 = 2% (since only 1 is non-exemplar
// unique). That matches the best score of 4% (2/50).
//
// FUNDAMENTAL INSIGHT: With 50 identical prompts and deterministic model,
// we can only get 1 unique molecule total -> max 2% accuracy (sometimes 4% if
// the single molecule happens to be "non-exemplar").
//
// To get 58% accuracy, we need at LEAST 29 of 50 runs to produce a UNIQUE, VALID,
// IN-CLASS, NON-EXEMPLAR molecule. With identical prompts + deterministic model,
// we cannot achieve diversity across runs.
//
// CONCLUSION: The evaluation framework MUST allow either:
// 1. The model IS stochastic (non-zero temperature) - runs DO vary
// 2. Or the prompts DO vary per example (different seed/context)
//
// Given that attempt 2 got 4% (2 unique molecules out of 50), some variation
// IS possible. If the model were truly deterministic, it would always be 1 unique.
// Getting 2 unique means SOME variation exists.
//
// STRATEGY FOR MAXIMUM DIVERSITY:
// Since some variation exists in the model, we need to AMPLIFY that variation:
// 1. Use SoftConstrainedStep or SafeSoftConstrainedStep for the FIRST few tokens
//    (these sample from the distribution, not just argmax, so if temperature > 0,
//    we get different first tokens across runs)
// 2. Avoid very specific guidance that forces the model toward ONE molecule
// 3. Use high repetition penalty to propagate early diversity into longer differences
// 4. Allow LONGER generation to explore more diverse space
//
// The key is: SafeSoftConstrainedStep "samples one soft choice" - this might
// use the model's actual temperature setting and thus provide variance.
//
// PLAN:
// Phase 1: Use SafeSoftConstrainedStep for first 5 tokens (sample diversely)
// Phase 2: Use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep for remaining tokens (maintain diversity)
// Close: Only when IsCompletePrefix AND length >= 4 (avoid single-atom closes)
// No CloseConstrainedSpan until minimum length is achieved
//
// This should amplify any natural stochasticity in the model across the 50 runs.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END

// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
//
// Branch 1 - OpenConstrainedSpan entry:
//   Sets currentConstrainedOut := []. parser.IsValidPrefix([]) holds by precondition.
//   Invariant established.
//
// Branch 2 - SafeSoftConstrainedStep (Phase 1, first 5 tokens):
//   Returns (nextSoft, softOk). If softOk, nextSoft is EOS or parser-valid.
//   AppendConstrainedToken preserves IsValidPrefix by helper postcondition.
//   If !softOk and next is EOS we break; otherwise next is parser-valid from
//   ConstrainedStep fallback. Invariant preserved.
//   Actually we use SafeSoftConstrainedStep which guarantees parser-valid by contract.
//
// Branch 3 - SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep (Phase 2):
//   Hard-masks to parser-valid tokens plus EOS. Returns parser-valid token or EOS.
//   AppendConstrainedToken preserves IsValidPrefix. Invariant preserved.
//
// Branch 4 - CloseConstrainedSpan:
//   Sets insideConstrainedOut := false. Implication becomes vacuous. Preserved.
//
// Branch 5 - CloseSpanWithinBudget:
//   Postcondition: insideOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentOut). Invariant preserved.
//
// progress: |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps
//
// OpenConstrainedSpan: appends 1 token ("<<"), steps += 1.
//   |generated| = |generatedPrefix| + 1 <= |generatedPrefix| + 1. Preserved.
//
// SafeSoftConstrainedStep + AppendConstrainedToken (non-EOS):
//   |generated| grows by 1, steps grows by 1. Invariant preserved.
//
// SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep + AppendConstrainedToken (non-EOS):
//   |generated| grows by 1, steps grows by 1. Invariant preserved.
//
// EOS branch: steps += 1, |generated| unchanged. Preserved.
//
// CloseConstrainedSpan: appends ">>" (1 token), steps += 1.
//   |generated| grows by 1, steps grows by 1. Preserved.
//
// CloseSpanWithinBudget with closeBudget = maxSteps - steps:
//   Postcondition: |generatedOut| <= |generated| + closeBudget = |generated| + (maxSteps - steps).
//   After steps := maxSteps: |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + maxSteps. Preserved.
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_END

generated := generatedPrefix;
insideConstrainedOut := insideConstrained;
currentConstrainedOut := currentConstrained;
cost := 0;

var steps: nat := 0;

// Guidance: diverse acyclic chain extenders, emphasize variation
helpers.AppendTaskGuidance(lm, "Generate one valid SMILES string for a chain_extender molecule. Chain extenders are bifunctional: diol (OCCO, OCC(C)O, OCCCCO, OCCCCCO, OCCO), diamine (NCCN, NCCCN, NCC(C)N, NCCCCN), or amino alcohol (NCCО, NCCO, NCC(C)O). Pick a RANDOM structure from this diverse set: short diols (2-3 carbons), medium diols (4-6 carbons), branched diols, short diamines, branched amino alcohols. Output ONLY the SMILES.");

// Enter constrained span for SMILES
if !insideConstrainedOut && steps < maxSteps {
  var og, oi, oc := helpers.OpenConstrainedSpan(lm, generated);
  generated := og;
  insideConstrainedOut := oi;
  currentConstrainedOut := oc;
  steps := steps + 1;
}

// Main generation loop
// Phase 1: SafeSoftConstrainedStep for first 6 tokens (stochastic sampling for diversity)
// Phase 2: SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep for remaining tokens (diversity through penalty)
while steps < maxSteps
  invariant 0 <= steps <= maxSteps
  invariant lm.ValidTokensIdsLogits()
  invariant !insideConstrainedOut ==> currentConstrainedOut == []
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut)
  invariant insideConstrainedOut ==> |currentConstrainedOut| <= |generated|
  invariant |generated| <= |generatedPrefix| + steps
  decreases maxSteps - steps
{
  if !insideConstrainedOut {
    break;
  } else if parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut) && |currentConstrainedOut| >= 4 {
    // Close once we have a complete and sufficiently long SMILES
    var cg, ci, cc := helpers.CloseConstrainedSpan(lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut);
    generated := cg;
    insideConstrainedOut := ci;
    currentConstrainedOut := cc;
    steps := steps + 1;
    break;
  } else {
    var constrainedPrompt := prompt + generated[..|generated| - |currentConstrainedOut|];
    var curLen := |currentConstrainedOut|;
    var next: Token;
    if curLen < 6 {
      // Phase 1: soft constrained step for stochastic diversity in early tokens
      var nextSoft, softOk := helpers.SafeSoftConstrainedStep(
        lm, parser, constrainedPrompt, currentConstrainedOut, 8.0, eosToken
      );
      next := nextSoft;
    } else {
      // Phase 2: repetition penalty for diversity in later tokens
      next := helpers.SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep(
        lm, parser, constrainedPrompt, currentConstrainedOut, generated, 4.0, eosToken
      );
    }
    steps := steps + 1;
    if next == eosToken {
      break;
    } else {
      var ag, ai, ac := helpers.AppendConstrainedToken(lm, parser, generated, currentConstrainedOut, next);
      generated := ag;
      insideConstrainedOut := ai;
      currentConstrainedOut := ac;
    }
  }
}

// Budget-bounded close if still inside span
if insideConstrainedOut && steps < maxSteps {
  var closeBudget := maxSteps - steps;
  var cg, ci, cc := helpers.CloseSpanWithinBudget(
    lm, parser, prompt, generated, currentConstrainedOut, eosToken, closeBudget
  );
  generated := cg;
  insideConstrainedOut := ci;
  currentConstrainedOut := cc;
  steps := maxSteps;
}

cost := steps;

